<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Philaret Drozdov</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1782">1782</a>–<a href="/years/1867">1867</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Vasily Mikhaylovich Drozdov, Metropolitan Philaret, Filaret</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Philaret, born Vasily Mikhaylovich Drozdov, was Metropolitan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolomna">Kolomna</a> and the most influential figure in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-orthodox-church">Russian Orthodox Church</a> from 1821 to 1867. Born in Kolomna to a clerical family, he taught at the Troitse-Sergiyeva Lavra and the academy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-nevsky">Alexander Nevsky</a> Lavra, became Archbishop of Tver in 1819, moved to Moscow in 1821 and became metropolitan in 1826. A leading preacher and author of many theological works, he worked toward a Russian translation of the Bible and is said to have prepared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-ii-of-russia">Alexander II</a>'s proclamation freeing the serfs; he also had Old Believer churches sealed and their clergy imprisoned. He was canonized in 1994.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Philaret Drozdov</b>, a cleric’s son who became a monk in 1808, headed the see of Moscow from 1821 to 1867 and was the church’s leading figure.</blockquote><a href="/societies/russian-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Russian Empire</a></li></ul></section>
